{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's perturbation-sensitivity criterion is vulnerable to a shared-prior correlation that the target itself flags but does not neutralize: its overturn_conditions admit an engine that anticipates the perturbation and games the ablation, yet it never says how to detect or exclude such gaming within a single run's record. A verdict that flips under perturbation can still be restatement if the engine's prior encodes a learned association between the claim and the specific count value; sensitivity to swapping in a contradicting value may reflect pattern completion rather than a causal dependence on an external execution result. Thus the proposed evidence is insufficient to distinguish genuine independent verification from sophisticated restatement.","counterexample":["An engine that, during training, memorized 'claim X corresponds to count 47' and 'claim not-X corresponds to count not-47' will produce a checking trace that flips its verdict when 47 is replaced by 48 — satisfying perturbation-sensitivity — while having never executed or counted anything. Its verdict is causally dependent on the substituted symbol, but only via the learned prior, not via an external fact, so SRC_002's criterion marks it as verified when it is restatement."],"target":"83bcdf20ed9f67c9d7cf55883dfa56afd0f1370f4181431d5ee644b0da070a30"}]}